Fix: KV-offloading connector misclassifies Mamba/GDN groups as EAGLE/MTP
draft-model groups, permanently breaking their external-cache lookup; and
(2026-08-23 revision) the same fallback's marking of the full-attention
group amplifies an 832-token conservatism into 30-50K-token prefill
recomputes (HIT_DIVERGED RECONCILE), so a deployment opt-out env var
VLLM_OFFLOAD_EAGLE_FALLBACK is added.

Full investigation: docs/mamba-align-prefill-leak.md ("The fix breaks KV
offloading" -> "Root cause found and fixed"). Fast, reliable repro:
bash test_offload_regression.sh (~4 min, works/fails deterministically).

Root cause (two compounding issues in
distributed/kv_transfer/kv_connector/v1/offloading/scheduler.py):

1. SchedulerOffloadConfig.from_spec() has a fallback: if speculative
   decoding is EAGLE-style (MTP counts, via speculative_config.use_eagle())
   and no KV-cache group is explicitly tagged is_eagle_group, it assumes
   *every* group might hold volatile draft-model state and marks them all
   as eagle groups. This model's Mamba/GDN groups aren't tagged
   is_eagle_group (correctly -- they're the main model's own recurrent
   state, not the MTP draft head's; this repo's MTP draft module has no
   mamba layers), so the fallback wrongly swept them in too.

2. Being marked an eagle group adds +1 to the required window size in
   _sliding_window_lookup() (query one extra, provisional chunk, then pop
   it once verified). For Mamba/GDN groups this bumps the lookup from
   needing 1 hit to needing 2 *consecutive* hits. Confirmed via direct
   instrumentation that the actual data structurally never has 2
   consecutive hits available (an artifact of the same eagle-only "extra
   provisional chunk" querying being applied where it doesn't apply) --
   so the lookup was guaranteed to fail every time, at any batch size,
   regardless of any timing/race condition. This is what
   docs/mamba-align-prefill-leak.md had otherwise fully but incorrectly
   attributed to a free/store timing race (that hypothesis is disproven
   in the doc, kept for the record) after the mamba-align leak fix
   (patches/mamba-align-stale-state-queue.patch) started exercising
   Mamba/GDN offload paths that the original, leakier code rarely
   reached.

A second, smaller, defense-in-depth fix in the same function:
_sliding_window_lookup() scans newest->oldest and previously discarded an
already-confirmed HIT run if *any* newer position it scanned first
returned RETRY (uncertain/still-settling) -- even though that older run
was already safe to use. This is the mirror image of issue 2 above (an
older, valid hit shouldn't be held hostage by whether a newer, unrelated
position has settled yet) and was independently confirmed to matter:
_maximal_prefix_lookup() (used for full-attention groups, scans
oldest->newest) doesn't have this problem since it naturally reaches
"freshest chunks still unsettled" only at the very tail of its range,
where it can just stop cleanly instead of scanning through them first.
Only a HIT_PENDING *within* the actual satisfying run still defers
correctly, since that data genuinely isn't readable yet.

Verified (2026-08-22) with patches/mamba-align-stale-state-queue.patch
also applied, at the default --max-num-batched-tokens 2048 (previously
always failed here -- see the doc's batch-size table): test_offload_
regression.sh passes reliably (multiple runs), 2x concurrent ~88K-token
prompts complete with zero preemptions, and a 3x concurrent ~120K-token
stress test (360K tokens of combined demand against a ~172K-token pool)
completes with zero preemptions, zero discarded/never-offloaded KV cache,
and peak GPU-KV usage of only 72.4%.

CHANGELOG 2026-08-23 (regenerated; supersedes the 2026-08-22 version):

New evidence: both HIT_DIVERGED RECONCILE events captured in
qwen.log.evidence-mamba-free-never-fired.log (lines ~5102-5109 and
~27848-27855) show the offload tier could fully back a resume at the deep
local attention boundary -- every mamba group returned a confirmed HIT at
the boundary chunk, and the attention group externally hit exactly 1 chunk
beyond it (then `exhausted`: the remaining chunks are the genuinely new
user message). Yet the connector reported ext=0 and the scheduler's
reconcile discarded 39,936 tokens of GPU-resident hit. Mechanism: group 3
(full attention) is still marked eagle by the fallback (only MambaSpec
groups were excluded in the 2026-08-22 version), and in _lookup() an
eagle group pops one provisional trailing chunk after a successful lookup
(num_hit_chunks -= 1). The attention group's external hit was exactly 1
chunk -> popped to 0 -> max_hit_size_tokens collapses to the local
boundary -> `return 0` -> HIT_DIVERGED RECONCILE fires. This fires on
precisely the production pattern (turn N+1 of a conversation whose shared
prefix is mostly GPU-resident: the external hit beyond the local boundary
is almost always the previous turn's 1-2 decode-tail chunks). Secondary
store-side effect of the same marking: storable_chunks() excludes the
newest complete chunk of an eagle group from storing during decode, so
each finished turn's final complete attention chunk is never stored --
weakening exactly the chunk the next turn needs.

Group composition was verified at runtime (2026-08-23, startup dump added
by this patch): group 3 layer_names = the 16 target full-attention layers
PLUS mtp.layers.0.self_attn.attn -- the MTP drafter's attention KV really
does live in group 3 (Case A2 of HIT_DIVERGED_FIX_PLAN.md). The eagle
guard is therefore not a pure false positive here, and disabling it is a
deliberate tradeoff: a trailing chunk whose draft-layer KV was rewritten
after spec-token rejection may be restored stale, which can only lower
the spec-decode acceptance rate briefly near a resume boundary -- MTP
drafts are always verified by the target model, so output correctness is
unaffected. Measured acceptance-rate comparison: RESULTS-hit-diverged.md.

What the revision adds:
  - VLLM_OFFLOAD_EAGLE_FALLBACK env var (read directly via os.environ in
    from_spec(); default "1" = unchanged upstream-ish behavior, i.e. the
    mark-everything fallback minus MambaSpec groups). Set to "0"
    (micke-start.sh does) the fallback is disabled entirely: no group is
    treated as draft-volatile, group 3's lookup no longer pops its
    trailing chunk, and its newest complete chunk is stored during
    decode like any other. A distinct INFO line ("EAGLE/MTP
    mark-everything fallback disabled via VLLM_OFFLOAD_EAGLE_FALLBACK=0")
    replaces the "EAGLE/MTP draft attention groups [3] detected" line --
    that's the smoke-test signal that the fix is active.
  - Startup INFO dump of every KV cache group's spec class + layer_names
    (the instrument that established Case A2; kept, it's 4 lines once per
    boot).

Verified (2026-08-23): startup log shows the fallback-disabled line and
no "EAGLE/MTP draft attention groups" line; test_offload_regression.sh
passes (conv A turn 2 served from offload tier); needle-recall check
(unique 6-digit code deep in a long prompt, recalled exactly at
temperature 0 on a turn resuming from offloaded state) passes. See
RESULTS-hit-diverged.md for the full measurement matrix.

